<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elipandus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Elipandus of Toledo, Elipando</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Elipandus was archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> in the late eighth century, when the city was under Muslim rule. He was the leading advocate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adoptionism">Adoptionism</a>, the teaching that Christ in his humanity was the adopted Son of God, which he defended in letters against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beatus-of-liebana">Beatus of Liébana</a> and the Frankish church. The doctrine was condemned at Regensburg in 792 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> in 794, but Elipandus never recanted.</p></div>
